export const RESOURCE_CATEGORIES = [
  'Getting started','Writing and publishing','Managing stories','Readers and engagement','Earnings and payments','Opportunities','Account and safety'
];

const RESOURCE_ARTICLE_DATA = [
{slug:'about-gidireads',category:'Getting started',title:'About GidiReads',summary:'Our story, mission and the values that shape the platform.',updated:'10 November 2026',body:`<p>GidiReads is a platform built for storytellers and the people who love their stories. Born in Africa and built for storytellers everywhere, we believe every voice deserves a place to be heard.</p><p>We provide tools for writers to publish, share, and grow their work — and for readers to discover new voices, follow their favourite authors, and build their own reading journeys.</p><h2>Our mission</h2><p>To make storytelling accessible to everyone, everywhere. Whether you're publishing your first chapter or your fiftieth novel, GidiReads gives you the tools to share your story with the world.</p><h2>Our values</h2><ul><li><strong>Creativity first.</strong> Every story matters.</li><li><strong>Accessibility.</strong> Reading and writing should be open to all.</li><li><strong>Community.</strong> Stories connect people.</li><li><strong>Independence.</strong> Writers keep control of their work.</li></ul>`},
{slug:'reading-library-and-lists',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Your library and reading lists',summary:'Save stories you want to return to and keep your reading organised.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Your Library helps you return to stories you have saved or started reading. Reading lists let you organise stories in a way that makes sense to you.</p><h2>Save a story</h2><p>Use the save or add-to-library control on a story page when it is available. Check your Library to make sure the story appears there.</p><h2>Create and manage lists</h2><p>Open the reading-list area and follow the controls to create a list, add stories or remove them. Choose a name that helps you remember what the list is for. If a list can be shared, check its visibility before sharing it.</p><h2>Continue reading</h2><p>Open a story from your Library or reading list. Your reading position may be saved when the reading progress feature is active. If you cannot find your place, open the chapter list and choose the chapter you last read.</p><h2>Saved does not mean owned</h2><p>Saving a story does not give you ownership of the writing or permission to copy, download, distribute or republish it.</p>`},
{slug:'daily-rewards-tasks-and-echoes',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Daily rewards and tasks',summary:'How daily claims, tasks and reward history work, and how to check what you received.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>GidiReads may offer daily rewards and tasks that give eligible members promotional benefits for using the platform.</p><h2>Daily claim</h2><p>Open the daily reward area and read the timer and claim message. A claim is available only when the page says it is ready. The timer shows when the next claim can be made. If the page shows a carry-over or streak rule, follow the rule displayed there.</p><h2>Tasks</h2><p>Open the task list and read the requirement before starting. Some tasks are completed by an action on GidiReads; others may need you to reach a stated milestone. A task is not complete until the platform confirms it.</p><h2>Reward history</h2><p>Check the history or wallet record to confirm what was awarded. Rewards may be promotional and can have separate rules from purchased balances. They are not cash and should not be treated as withdrawable earnings.</p><h2>A task did not count</h2><p>Check that you completed the exact action and that the task is still active. Allow a short time for the record to update. If it remains missing, contact support with the task name and the date you completed it.</p>`},
{slug:'badges-milestones-and-achievements',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Badges, milestones and achievements',summary:'What profile badges and milestones recognise and how to check your progress.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Badges and milestones recognise certain activities or achievements on GidiReads. The requirements depend on the badge or milestone.</p><h2>Earn a badge</h2><p>Open the badge or achievement area to read the requirement. Some badges may be awarded automatically after the platform confirms an activity. Others may be connected to a contest, task or membership.</p><h2>Show or hide badges</h2><p>If your profile offers a badge-visibility setting, use it to choose which earned badges other people can see. Hiding a badge does not mean you have lost it.</p><h2>Membership badges</h2><p>A badge that records an achievement you have earned is different from a badge that shows an active subscription or membership. Check the label and membership terms to understand what it represents.</p><h2>Progress has not updated</h2><p>Some achievements are checked after an action is recorded. If your progress does not change, review the requirement and wait for the activity to sync. Contact support if the record still appears incorrect.</p>`},
{slug:'creators-and-adaptation-opportunities',category:'Opportunities',title:'Creators and adaptation opportunities',summary:'Understand the creator role and how adaptation opportunities differ from writing and publishing.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>On GidiReads, an author writes and publishes stories. A creator is someone interested in finding stories that may be suitable for an adaptation project, such as a screen, audio or other creative adaptation.</p><h2>Being a creator</h2><p>Creator access and verification may be required before some creator features are available. Follow the verification instructions shown in your account. Never send passwords or sign-in codes to someone claiming to verify you.</p><h2>Find an opportunity</h2><p>Read the full opportunity details, including the type of adaptation being considered, eligibility, rights requested, selection process and deadline. Do not assume that viewing a story or contacting its author gives you adaptation rights.</p><h2>Rights and permission</h2><p>Story rights remain with the person or organisation that owns them unless a valid agreement says otherwise. An adaptation must not begin until the necessary rights and permissions have been agreed in writing by the people who can grant them.</p><h2>Authors</h2><p>If someone approaches you about adapting your story, check who they are, what they want to make, which rights they need, how long they need them and what payment or credit is offered. Do not accept an unclear offer.</p>`},
{slug:'contract-centre-application',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Apply through the Contract Centre',summary:'What the contract application asks for and what happens after you submit it.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open the Contract Centre from Authors Studio. The application is about a specific story, not a request to monetise every story on your account.</p><h2>Prepare before you begin</h2><p>Make sure the story details and chapters are up to date. You may be asked about the story, its publication history, your rights, readership, plans and readiness for a contract review.</p><h2>Answer the questions carefully</h2><p>Give honest and complete answers. If a question does not apply, use the option provided or explain why. Do not guess figures or claim rights you do not have.</p><h2>Review and submit</h2><p>Read your answers before submitting. The application page explains that applying does not guarantee acceptance or an offer. The GidiReads team reviews the story and decides whether an offer is appropriate.</p><h2>After you submit</h2><p>Check the Contract Centre for your application status and any offer or request for more information. Read the full terms before accepting. You can ask questions before making a decision.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-paid-chapter-access',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Paid chapters and chapter access',summary:'How paid chapter access is intended to work and what is currently unavailable.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Some stories may be approved for paid chapter access under a specific creator agreement. A price shown on a chapter does not by itself mean that checkout is active.</p><h2>Before unlocking</h2><p>When paid access is active, the story page should show which chapter is paid and the G Coin price. Review the amount and confirm that you want to unlock that chapter.</p><h2>Current availability</h2><p>Real-money purchases and paid chapter checkout are not active until GidiReads has connected and tested its payment provider. Do not send money outside the platform to unlock a chapter.</p><h2>Already unlocked?</h2><p>When purchase records are active, an eligible chapter you have already unlocked should remain available under the published rules. If access is missing, check that you are signed into the same account and contact support with the story and chapter name.</p>`},
{slug:'language-currency-and-regional-settings',category:'Getting started',title:'Language, currency and regional information',summary:'How to understand language and currency displays while GidiReads expands to more regions.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>GidiReads is being built for readers and writers in different places. Some parts of the platform may show regional settings or currency information as those features become available.</p><h2>Language</h2><p>If a language selector is shown, choose the language you want to use and check whether the page updates. A language setting may not translate every story, comment or user-written post.</p><h2>Currency</h2><p>Use the currency clearly shown beside a price or payment term. Do not assume that every displayed amount has been converted to your local currency. Check the official terms before making a payment or accepting a payout.</p><h2>Regional availability</h2><p>Payment methods, payout options and some opportunities may depend on location and local requirements. If a feature is not available in your region, follow the notice on the page rather than using another person's payment details.</p>`},
{slug:'verification-and-account-eligibility',category:'Account and safety',title:'Verification and account eligibility',summary:'Why a feature may ask you to verify details and how to complete checks safely.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Some GidiReads features may be limited to accounts that meet specific requirements. A verification check helps the platform confirm that an account is eligible for that feature.</p><h2>Follow the official process</h2><p>Start verification only from the relevant GidiReads page. Read what information is requested and why it is needed. If the page does not explain the request, pause and contact the platform team.</p><h2>Protect your information</h2><p>Never share your password, one-time code or account recovery link. Do not send identity or payment documents to an unofficial social media account or personal number.</p><h2>Pending or declined checks</h2><p>If a check is pending, wait for the status to update. If it is declined, read the reason and any appeal instructions. Do not create duplicate accounts to get around an eligibility decision.</p>`},
{slug:'set-chapter-prices',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Set chapter prices for a contracted story',summary:'Choose automatic or manual pricing and keep every chapter within your contract limit.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Chapter pricing is only available for a story with an active non-exclusive or exclusive contract. Echo-only stories and stories without an eligible contract cannot use paid chapter pricing.</p><h2>Open the right story</h2><p>Open Authors Studio, go to My Stories and open the story you want to price. In that story's Workspace, choose Chapter Pricing. The setting is for that story only.</p><h2>Choose who sets the price</h2><p>You can let GidiReads set prices based on chapter length, or choose to set each price yourself. You can change this choice later. Automatic prices are calculated on the server and stay within your contract limit.</p><h2>Know your price limit</h2><p>Non-exclusive stories can use prices from 25 to 60 G Coins per paid chapter. Exclusive stories can use prices from 25 to 100 G Coins per paid chapter. Manual prices outside the allowed range will not save.</p><h2>Keep chapters free or make them paid</h2><p>You choose which chapters are free and which chapters readers must unlock. Review the chapter and its price before saving.</p><h2>Important: purchases are not active yet</h2><p>Saving a chapter price does not turn on checkout. Paid chapter purchases remain unavailable until GidiReads connects and tests its payment system. Readers must not be asked to pay outside GidiReads.</p>`},
{slug:'first-time-on-gidireads',category:'Getting started',title:'Getting started on GidiReads',summary:'A clear first guide to your account, your profile and the main areas of the platform.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Welcome to GidiReads. You can use the platform to read stories, publish your own work, meet readers and take part in opportunities when they are available.</p><h2>Set up your account</h2><ol><li>Create an account with an email address you can access.</li><li>Choose a username. This is how people can find and mention you on GidiReads.</li><li>Complete the profile details you want other members to see.</li><li>Keep your sign-in details private. GidiReads staff will never need your password.</li></ol><h2>Find your way around</h2><p>Use <a href="explore.html">Explore</a> to discover stories, <a href="library.html">Library</a> to return to stories you have saved, and your profile to manage your public information. If you publish stories, open <a href="authors-studio.html">Authors Studio</a> for your writing tools.</p><h2>Reader or author?</h2><p>You do not need to publish a story to enjoy GidiReads. You can begin as a reader and use the writing tools when you are ready. Publishing access is available through Authors Studio for accounts that can use author features.</p><h2>Something looks different?</h2><p>Some features may appear only to eligible accounts or while a programme is open. If a page says a feature is unavailable, do not assume you have done something wrong. Check the notice on that page and contact support if the message does not explain what to do.</p>`},
{slug:'your-profile-and-settings',category:'Getting started',title:'Your profile and account settings',summary:'What your profile shows, what you can change and how to keep your account details safe.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Your profile is your public identity on GidiReads. It helps readers recognise you and find your work.</p><h2>Update your profile</h2><p>Open your account or profile page to review the details available to you, such as your display name, username, picture and biography. Save changes before leaving the page. If a field is not available, it may not be supported by the current version.</p><h2>Public and private information</h2><p>Only add information you are comfortable sharing. Your public profile is different from private account information used to manage your account. Do not put passwords, verification codes, payment details or private contact information in your biography.</p><h2>Changing your username</h2><p>If username editing is available in your account settings, follow the instructions shown there. A username may need to be unique. If your chosen name is already in use, choose another one.</p><h2>Keep your account safe</h2><p>Use a password you do not use elsewhere, sign out on shared devices and never share a one-time code. If you think someone else has accessed your account, change your password and contact the platform team.</p>`},
{slug:'create-your-first-story',category:'Writing and publishing',title:'Create your first story',summary:'How to start a novel and complete the information readers need before they open it.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open Authors Studio and choose <a href="create-story.html">Create Story</a>. Work through the steps and save your details as you go.</p><h2>Choose a clear title</h2><p>Use the title readers should see on the story page. Check spelling before publishing. You can update story details later if the editing controls allow it.</p><h2>Write a useful synopsis</h2><p>Tell readers what the story is about, who or what it follows and what makes the situation interesting. Keep important twists and the ending private. Avoid promises that the story does not deliver.</p><h2>Choose genres and tags carefully</h2><p>Select the genres that best describe the story. Add tags that help the right readers find it. Do not add unrelated popular tags just to attract clicks.</p><h2>Add a cover</h2><p>Use artwork you created, licensed or have permission to use. Make sure the title and important details remain readable at a small size. Do not use another author's cover or artwork without permission.</p><h2>Save and review</h2><p>Check the title, synopsis, genre, tags and cover before you publish. A draft is not the same as a published story. Open My Stories to find your saved work and continue editing.</p>`},
{slug:'write-and-format-chapters',category:'Writing and publishing',title:'Write and format chapters',summary:'Prepare chapters so they are comfortable to read on phones, tablets and computers.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open <a href="my-stories.html">My Stories</a>, choose the story you want to work on and open its writing or chapter workspace.</p><h2>Give each chapter a clear heading</h2><p>Use a chapter title or number that matches the order of your story. Check that you are editing the correct chapter before saving.</p><h2>Make the text easy to read</h2><ul><li>Break long passages into paragraphs.</li><li>Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes.</li><li>Use punctuation consistently.</li><li>Avoid repeated spaces, decorative symbols and excessive capital letters.</li><li>Preview the chapter before publishing.</li></ul><h2>Save and publish</h2><p>Save your work before leaving the editor. If the chapter is still a draft, readers will not see it as a published chapter. Use the publish control only after checking the text and chapter order.</p><h2>If your work does not save</h2><p>Check your connection and wait for any saving message to finish. Copy your latest writing somewhere safe before refreshing if you are unsure whether it was saved. If the problem continues, note the story and chapter name when contacting support.</p>`},
{slug:'story-workspace-and-metadata',category:'Managing stories',title:'Use Story Workspace and story details',summary:'Where to manage your story information, notes, planning material and chapter work.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Story Workspace brings together the tools connected to an individual story. The exact tools shown can depend on the story and the features available to your account.</p><h2>Story details</h2><p>Use the story details or metadata area to review information such as the title, synopsis, genre, tags and cover. Keep these details accurate as the story changes.</p><h2>Planning tools</h2><p>Story Bible and Author Notes are for your own planning and reference. Use them to keep character details, setting notes, plot points and reminders together. They are not a substitute for saving your actual chapter text.</p><h2>Chapter work</h2><p>Use the chapter area to create, edit, arrange and publish chapters. Check the chapter list after making changes so the reading order is correct.</p><h2>Bulk chapter import</h2><p>If a bulk import option is available in your Story Workspace, read its on-screen instructions before selecting files. Check the imported chapter titles and order carefully. Do not assume an import has published chapters unless the page confirms that it has.</p><h2>Keep a separate copy</h2><p>Keep your own copy of important manuscripts and notes. A platform account should not be your only copy of work that matters to you.</p>`},
{slug:'edit-unpublish-and-delete-stories',category:'Managing stories',title:'Edit, unpublish or remove a story',summary:'Understand the difference between editing a story, hiding it from readers and removing it.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Before making a major change, open the correct story and read the confirmation message on the page.</p><h2>Edit a story</h2><p>Editing changes the story information or chapter text. Review the saved result after making changes, especially if readers have already seen the earlier version.</p><h2>Unpublish or hide a story</h2><p>If an unpublish or visibility control is available, use it when you need to stop new readers from accessing the story. The page should explain what happens to existing readers and saved links. If it does not, ask support before proceeding.</p><h2>Delete a story</h2><p>Deletion may be difficult or impossible to reverse. Read the confirmation carefully and keep a copy of your manuscript first. Do not delete a story simply to correct a typo; edit the story instead.</p><h2>Rights and responsibility</h2><p>You are responsible for having the rights to publish the story and its cover. If a rights concern is raised, respond through the process explained in the notice you receive.</p>`},
{slug:'readership-and-story-statistics',category:'Managing stories',title:'Understand your readership and story statistics',summary:'What story activity can tell you and why a number may not change immediately.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Story statistics help you understand how readers are finding and engaging with your work. The information shown depends on which tracking features are active.</p><h2>Reads and views</h2><p>A view or read count is an activity measure. It does not tell you whether a reader finished the story or enjoyed it. Different pages may show different measures, so use the label beside each number.</p><h2>Likes, comments and Echoes</h2><p>These actions show different kinds of engagement. A like is a quick sign of appreciation. A comment is a written response. An Echo is a form of support. Do not treat one as a replacement for another.</p><h2>Why numbers may differ</h2><p>Some statistics update after a delay. Counts may also be filtered to remove invalid or repeated activity. If a number looks wrong for a long time, record the story title and the figure you see, then contact support.</p><h2>Use the information well</h2><p>Look for patterns over time rather than judging a story by one day's numbers. You can use reader feedback to decide what to improve, but do not feel pressured to change a story just to chase a metric.</p>`},
{slug:'likes-comments-and-following',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Likes, comments and following authors',summary:'How readers show appreciation and keep up with stories and writers.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>GidiReads has different ways to help readers respond to stories and stay connected with writers.</p><h2>Story likes</h2><p>Use the like control on a story when you want to show that you appreciate it. If you tap it again and the page allows it, your like may be removed. A like is not a paid action.</p><h2>Comments</h2><p>Use comments to share a thoughtful response or ask a relevant question. Keep discussion respectful. Do not post personal information, harassment, threats or material that breaks the platform rules. Report content that concerns you rather than starting an argument.</p><h2>Following</h2><p>Follow an author or use the available story-saving tools to make it easier to return to work you enjoy. Following does not give you permission to copy, republish or distribute an author's work.</p><h2>Notifications</h2><p>Check Notifications for activity and updates. Some alerts may depend on your settings, the feature involved and whether notifications are enabled on your device.</p>`},
{slug:'echoes-and-supporting-authors',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Echoes and supporting authors',summary:'What Echoes mean, how to send them and what readers should check first.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Echoes are GidiReads' way for readers to show support for authors. They are different from G Coins, which are used for paid chapter access when that feature is enabled.</p><h2>Sending an Echo</h2><p>Where the Echo control is available, choose whether you want to support a chapter or the whole novel if the page offers both choices. Review the amount and recipient before confirming. The page should show the result after a successful action.</p><h2>Free and purchased Echoes</h2><p>GidiReads may provide promotional or daily Echoes under the rules shown in the wallet or reward area. These are different from purchased balances. Check the balance type and any expiry or carry-over information displayed before using them.</p><h2>Can I Echo a chapter again?</h2><p>Follow the limits and eligibility message shown by the Echo control. If the platform says a chapter is eligible, you can use the available options. If it says you have already supported it or are not eligible, do not try to work around the limit.</p><h2>Check your activity</h2><p>Review your wallet or activity history when available. If an Echo is missing or appears more than once, do not repeat the action. Contact support with the story, chapter and approximate time.</p>`},
{slug:'gifts-and-gift-catalogue',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Send a gift to an author',summary:'Find a gift, choose the right author and check the details before sending.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Gifts let readers send an item of appreciation to an author through the gift catalogue.</p><h2>Choose a gift</h2><p>Open the gift area from the place where gifting is offered. Review the gift name, image, description and Echo cost before choosing it. Gift costs are shown in Echoes, not G Coins. Gifts use Purchased Support Echoes, not G Coins, free appreciation Echoes or promotional Echoes.</p><h2>Choose the recipient</h2><p>Search for the author's GidiReads username or display name. Check the result carefully so you do not send a gift to the wrong person. If the author does not appear, check the spelling and try the username.</p><h2>Confirm the gift</h2><p>Review the recipient and cost before sending. A successful send should show a confirmation. If the request fails, check your balance and connection before trying again.</p><h2>Gift problems</h2><p>If your balance changed but the gift is not shown, do not send it again straight away. Save the error message and contact support with the recipient name and gift details.</p>`},
{slug:'coins-pass-and-wallet',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'G Coins, GidiPass and your wallet',summary:"A simple explanation of the platform's reading currency, access pass and wallet records.",updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Your wallet may show different balances and records. Each has a separate purpose.</p><h2>G Coins</h2><p>G Coins are intended for paid chapter access where paid chapters have been approved and the payment system is active. They are not the same as Echoes.</p><h2>Echoes</h2><p>Echoes are used to support authors and send gifts where those features are available. A free or promotional Echo reward is not the same as a purchased balance.</p><h2>GidiPass</h2><p>GidiPass is an access product for the benefits described on its purchase or membership page. Always read the current terms and duration before subscribing.</p><h2>Current payment availability</h2><p>Real-money purchases, paid chapter checkout, subscriptions and cash payouts are not active until GidiReads has connected and tested its payment provider and published the relevant terms. A balance or pricing screen does not mean a real-money payment is currently available. Do not send money to anyone claiming they can activate a wallet manually.</p><h2>Wallet history</h2><p>When transaction history is available, use it to review credits, debits and their descriptions. If you see an unfamiliar entry, contact support rather than trying to change your balance yourself.</p>`},
{slug:'contracts-and-paid-chapters',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Contracts, paid chapters and your rights',summary:'What a GidiReads contract means and what to check before accepting an offer.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>A contract is a written agreement between the author and GidiReads for the specific story and rights described in that agreement. A story is not contracted simply because it is popular or because an offer is discussed.</p><h2>Read the whole offer</h2><p>Check the story covered by the contract, the rights requested, the term, payment terms, responsibilities, termination rules and how disputes are handled. Ask questions about anything you do not understand before accepting.</p><h2>Contracts are story-specific</h2><p>Do not assume an agreement for one novel automatically covers your other stories. Check the exact title and scope in the contract.</p><h2>Paid chapter access</h2><p>Only stories approved for paid-chapter monetisation with the correct contract scope should have paid chapter settings. Even then, real-money purchases remain unavailable until the payment system is activated and tested.</p><h2>Your rights</h2><p>Do not sign away rights you do not intend to grant. If you are unsure what a clause means, seek independent advice before signing. Keep a copy of the final agreement and any written changes.</p>`},
{slug:'earnings-and-payouts',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Creator earnings and payouts',summary:'Where earnings records belong and what must be ready before withdrawals can begin.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>The Creator Earnings area is intended to show eligible earnings, ledger records and payout requests for creators who have an active agreement.</p><h2>Who can earn?</h2><p>Earnings depend on an approved monetisation arrangement, the activity covered by that arrangement and the terms in your contract. Having an author account alone does not create an entitlement to payment.</p><h2>Review your records</h2><p>When the feature is active for your account, check the period, source and status of each entry. Keep your own records and report entries you do not recognise.</p><h2>Withdrawals</h2><p>Cash payouts are not active until GidiReads has configured a payment provider, verified the payout process and published payout rules. Do not submit bank details through an unofficial message or pay a person to release your earnings.</p><h2>Currency</h2><p>Use the currency and conversion information shown in the official payout terms when they are published. Do not assume a displayed estimate is a confirmed amount you can withdraw.</p>`},
{slug:'find-and-apply-for-opportunities',category:'Opportunities',title:'Find and apply for opportunities',summary:'How to read an opportunity, check whether you qualify and submit an application.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open <a href="opportunities.html">Opportunities</a> from Authors Studio to see programmes currently listed for authors and creators.</p><h2>Read the full details</h2><p>Open an opportunity and check its purpose, who can apply, opening and closing dates, required materials, word limits, rights terms and selection process. Do not rely only on the short card summary.</p><h2>Check eligibility</h2><p>Make sure your account and story meet every listed requirement before applying. If a requirement is unclear, ask the programme team before the deadline.</p><h2>Submit your application</h2><p>Complete every required field, attach or select the requested work and review your answers. Submit before the closing time shown on the opportunity page. Keep the confirmation or application status for your records.</p><h2>After applying</h2><p>Check the opportunity page and your notifications for updates. A submitted application is not the same as acceptance. The programme team may review, request information or update the status.</p>`},
{slug:'holiday-hearts-entry-guide',category:'Opportunities',title:'Holiday Hearts: preparing and submitting an entry',summary:'A practical guide to the Holiday Hearts story contest and its entry requirements.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Holiday Hearts is a GidiReads writing opportunity. The live opportunity page is the final source for its current dates, prompt, word limits, eligibility rules and entry status. Always read that page before preparing your submission.</p><h2>Prepare your story</h2><p>Read the current prompt and judging information. Make sure the story fits the theme and that you own or control the rights needed to submit it. Check the minimum and maximum word counts shown on the opportunity.</p><h2>Submit through the entry flow</h2><p>The entry flow allows one entry per author. If you need to correct something, contact the programme team instead of creating another entry. Use the entry action on the Holiday Hearts opportunity page. The submission flow may ask you to create or select a story, provide a title and synopsis, confirm the required rights statements and add chapters. Follow the instructions shown in the form.</p><h2>Before you submit</h2><ul><li>Check the title and synopsis.</li><li>Check that the correct chapters are included and in the right order.</li><li>Make sure the entry meets the current word limit.</li><li>Read and confirm every required eligibility and rights statement honestly.</li><li>Submit before the deadline and look for a confirmation.</li></ul><h2>After submission</h2><p>Use the opportunity page to check your entry status. If entries are reviewed, shortlisted or selected as finalists, the status should be updated through the programme process. Finalists and winners are determined by the published rules, not by private messages.</p><h2>Need to change an entry?</h2><p>Do not create a second entry to replace the first unless the opportunity page explicitly allows it. Contact the programme team and include your entry title and account username.</p>`},
{slug:'opportunity-status-and-results',category:'Opportunities',title:'Application status, finalists and results',summary:'What common application stages mean and where to look for official decisions.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Your opportunity application may move through several stages. The exact labels and process depend on the programme.</p><h2>Submitted</h2><p>Your application has been sent. This does not mean it has been approved.</p><h2>Under review</h2><p>The programme team is checking the application against its requirements. You may need to wait for an update.</p><h2>Approved or accepted</h2><p>The application has passed the relevant review stage. Read any next steps carefully; approval may still require you to complete another action.</p><h2>Finalist</h2><p>For a contest that uses finalists, this means the entry has been selected for the finalist stage under the published process. It does not by itself mean the entry has won.</p><h2>Winner or placement</h2><p>Final results should be published through the official opportunity page or an official GidiReads announcement. Check the listed placements and any instructions for winners.</p><h2>Appeals or questions</h2><p>Use the appeal or contact route described in the opportunity rules. Include the opportunity name, entry title and a clear explanation. Keep your message respectful and do not share private application details publicly.</p>`},
{slug:'notifications-and-announcements',category:'Getting started',title:'Notifications and announcements',summary:'Where to find account alerts and official platform updates.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Notifications help you keep track of activity connected to your account. Announcements share information from GidiReads.</p><h2>Open Notifications</h2><p>Use the notification bell or Notifications page. Unread alerts may be marked differently from alerts you have already opened. If a mark-as-read control is shown, use it after reviewing the alert.</p><h2>Push notifications</h2><p>Push alerts depend on browser or device permission, a supported browser and an active GidiReads notification service. Allow notifications only on a device you trust. You can change permission in your browser or device settings.</p><h2>Official announcements</h2><p>Check the announcement itself for its date, audience and any links. GidiReads will not ask you to share your password or verification code in an announcement.</p><h2>You did not receive an alert</h2><p>Open the Notifications page directly and check your device permissions. Some messages may not be sent as push alerts. The official page or announcement remains the place to confirm important information.</p>`},
{slug:'communities-and-conduct',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Communities and respectful participation',summary:'How to take part in discussions and what to do when a community interaction goes wrong.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Communities are places to discuss stories, writing and shared interests. Each community may have its own topic and rules.</p><h2>Take part thoughtfully</h2><p>Stay on topic, respect different opinions and give useful feedback. Criticise ideas or writing choices without attacking the person who made them.</p><h2>Protect your privacy</h2><p>Do not post passwords, private addresses, financial details, private conversations or another person's personal information. Ask before sharing someone else's work or messages.</p><h2>Report a concern</h2><p>Use the report option when a post or account breaks the rules or makes you feel unsafe. Include a clear reason. Do not retaliate or encourage others to target the person.</p><h2>Moderation</h2><p>Moderators may review reports and take action under the platform rules. A report is a request for review, not a promise that a particular outcome will follow.</p>`},
{slug:'privacy-security-and-reporting',category:'Account and safety',title:'Privacy, account security and reporting',summary:'Protect your information and report content or behaviour that concerns you.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Keep your account and personal information safe while using GidiReads.</p><h2>Never share sign-in codes</h2><p>Do not give anyone your password, one-time code or account recovery link. Staff should not ask you to send these details in a chat or email.</p><h2>Think before you post</h2><p>Information on a public profile, story, comment or community post may be seen by other people. Do not publish private information about yourself or anyone else.</p><h2>Report content or an account</h2><p>Use the report control where it is available. Choose the closest reason and explain what happened. Include the relevant story, chapter, comment or username so the team can find it.</p><h2>What happens next?</h2><p>The moderation team reviews reports under the platform rules. It may need time or more information. Do not assume that a report has been resolved until you receive an update or can see the result.</p><h2>Account access problem</h2><p>If you cannot sign in, use the account recovery option on the sign-in page. If you think your account has been taken over, secure your email account too and contact GidiReads through an official channel.</p>`},
{slug:'troubleshooting-common-problems',category:'Account and safety',title:'Fix common problems on GidiReads',summary:'Simple checks for pages that do not load, changes that do not save and actions that fail.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<h2>A page keeps loading</h2><p>Check your internet connection, refresh once and try again. If the problem continues, note the page name and time. Do not repeatedly submit the same form while it is still processing.</p><h2>Your story or chapter is missing</h2><p>Check that you are signed into the correct account and open My Stories. Confirm whether the story is a draft, published or hidden. If it is still missing, contact support with the title.</p><h2>Your changes did not save</h2><p>Wait for the save message. If you are unsure, copy your latest text before refreshing. Reopen the page and check whether the change appears.</p><h2>A gift or Echo did not go through</h2><p>Check your balance and activity history before trying again. If the balance changed but the action is missing, keep the error message and contact support.</p><h2>You cannot apply for an opportunity</h2><p>Check the opening and closing dates, account eligibility, word count and required fields. If you believe you qualify but the form still blocks you, contact the programme team before the deadline.</p><h2>What to include when asking for help</h2><p>Tell us the page you were using, what you were trying to do, what happened instead and the exact error message if one appeared. Never include your password, verification code or full payment details.</p>`}

,
{slug:'reader-create-account',category:'Getting started',title:'Create an account or log in',summary:'How to join GidiReads, sign in again and get back into your account.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>You can read public stories without publishing anything. An account lets you follow writers, save stories and use the features that need a signed-in member.</p><h2>Create an account</h2><ol><li>Open the GidiReads home page and choose Log in.</li><li>Choose the option to create an account.</li><li>Enter an email address you can open and choose a username and password.</li><li>Follow any email confirmation step shown on the page.</li><li>Return to GidiReads and log in.</li></ol><h2>Log in again</h2><p>Choose Log in and enter the email and password linked to your account. If you are using a shared phone or computer, do not save your password in the browser.</p><h2>You cannot remember your password</h2><p>Choose Forgot password on the sign-in form. Enter your account email and check your inbox, including the spam folder. Use the reset link sent by GidiReads. If no message arrives, check that the email address is correct and try again later.</p><h2>Still cannot get in?</h2><p>Do not create another account just to get around a sign-in problem. Contact GidiReads support and tell us the username and the step where you get stuck. Never send your password or a reset code.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-find-stories',category:'Getting started',title:'Find stories to read',summary:'Use Explore to look for stories by title, genre and the details shown on each story card.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open <a href="explore.html">Explore</a> to browse stories on GidiReads. You can look through the collections on the page or use the search and filters that are available.</p><h2>Search for a story</h2><p>Enter a title, author name or a word that describes the kind of story you want. If the result list is too broad, try a more specific word or choose a genre filter.</p><h2>Check the story before opening it</h2><p>Read the title, synopsis, genre and tags. These details help you decide whether the story is a good match. A cover or a high read count does not guarantee that you will enjoy a story.</p><h2>Open the story</h2><p>Select the story card to open its page. Read the description and check the chapter list before you begin. If a story is not available, it may have been unpublished, removed or limited by its author or the platform.</p><h2>You cannot find a story</h2><p>Check the spelling of the title or author's username. Try a shorter search. If you have its direct link and it no longer opens, the story may no longer be public.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-read-and-change-chapters',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Open a story and move between chapters',summary:'How to start reading, choose another chapter and return to a story later.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open a story from Explore, a writer's profile or your Library. On the story page, read the synopsis and open the chapter list.</p><h2>Start reading</h2><p>Choose the first available chapter or the chapter you want to read. Use the reading controls on the page to move to the next or previous chapter when they are available.</p><h2>Choose a different chapter</h2><p>Open the chapter list and select the chapter title. Check the chapter number before opening it so you do not skip part of the story by mistake.</p><h2>Return later</h2><p>Add the story to your Library or a reading list so you can find it again. If your reading position is not restored automatically, open the chapter list and choose the last chapter you remember reading.</p><h2>A chapter will not open</h2><p>Check that you are signed in if the page asks you to do so. If the chapter is marked as paid, read the paid-access notice. Paid checkout is not active until GidiReads announces that payments are ready. Do not pay someone outside the platform for access.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-follow-like-and-comment',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'When likes, follows or comments do not work',summary:'Simple checks to try when an interaction does not save or appear on the page.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>If a like, follow or comment does not appear, check the page before trying the action again.</p><h2>A like did not change</h2><p>Make sure the page finished loading and check whether the like count or button changed. Refresh the story page once. If the like still does not appear, sign in again and try later.</p><h2>A follow did not save</h2><p>Open the writer's public profile and check the button. If it still says Follow, try once more when your connection is stable. If it says Following, the action has already been saved.</p><h2>A comment is missing</h2><p>Check whether the comment form showed a success message. If it did not, copy your comment before refreshing, then check your connection and try again. A comment may also be hidden while it is reviewed under the platform rules.</p><h2>Keep interactions respectful</h2><p>Do not post threats, harassment or private information. If content concerns you, use the report option instead of starting an argument.</p><h2>Still not working?</h2><p>Note the story title, the action you tried and any message shown on the page. Contact support with those details. Never send your password or sign-in code.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-send-echoes-and-gifts',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'When an Echo or gift does not go through',summary:'What to check before you try again if an Echo or gift fails.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Do not repeat an Echo or gift straight away if the page shows an error. First check whether the action was recorded.</p><h2>Check the balance and activity</h2><p>Open My GidiReads and review the wallet or activity record. Look for a new entry that matches the amount and the time of your action.</p><h2>If the balance did not change</h2><p>Check your internet connection and whether the feature is available on that page. Try again only after the page has loaded normally and you are sure the first request did not go through.</p><h2>If the balance changed but the action is missing</h2><p>Do not send it again. Save the error message, note the story or chapter, and contact support with the approximate time and transaction details.</p><h2>If you chose the wrong recipient</h2><p>Contact support as soon as possible with the recipient username, gift name and transaction details. Do not ask another member to share private account information.</p><h2>Remember</h2><p>Gift costs are shown in Echoes, not G Coins. Never pay another member or send your password to someone who offers to fix a failed transaction.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-wallet-and-activity',category:'Earnings and payments',title:'Check your wallet and activity',summary:'Where to review your balances and what to do if a wallet record looks wrong.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open <a href="my-gidireads.html">My GidiReads</a> and look for the wallet and activity records shown on your account.</p><h2>Understand the balance names</h2><p>G Coins are intended for paid chapter access when that service is active. Echoes are used to support writers. GidiPass is an access or membership feature. These balances and benefits have different rules, so check the label beside each one.</p><h2>Check a recent record</h2><p>Read the date, description and amount. If you do not recognise an entry, do not try to change the balance yourself. Save the details and contact support.</p><h2>Your balance changed but the action is missing</h2><p>Do not repeat the action straight away. Check the activity list and keep any reference number or error message. Send those details to support so the team can look into it.</p><h2>Can you withdraw a wallet balance?</h2><p>Wallet balances are not the same as creator earnings. Do not assume that G Coins or Echoes can be withdrawn as cash.</p><h2>Payments are not active yet</h2><p>Real-money purchases and paid chapter checkout remain unavailable until GidiReads connects and tests its payment provider. Do not send money to another member or staff account to top up a balance.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-notifications-and-updates',category:'Getting started',title:'Fix notification problems',summary:'What to check when an alert is missing, unread or does not open.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Open <a href="notifications.html">Notifications</a> to check whether the alert is in your account.</p><h2>The unread count looks wrong</h2><p>Open the Notifications page and check the list itself. If an alert is still marked unread, use the mark-as-read control when it is shown. Refresh the page if the count does not update.</p><h2>An alert will not open</h2><p>Open the notification again after checking your internet connection. If its link no longer works, the story or page may have changed. Use Explore or the writer's profile to find the item again.</p><h2>You did not receive a push alert</h2><p>Check whether your browser or device allows notifications for GidiReads. Push alerts also depend on the platform notification service being active. You can still open Notifications directly even when push alerts are off.</p><h2>You did not receive an announcement</h2><p>Announcements may be sent only to selected members. Check the audience and date shown on the announcement. Not every announcement is sent as a push alert.</p><h2>Still missing?</h2><p>Note the type of alert you expected and when it should have arrived. Contact support if it is important and does not appear in your Notifications page.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-join-community',category:'Readers and engagement',title:'Join a GidiReads community',summary:'How to take part in community spaces and help keep them welcoming.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Communities give members a place to talk about stories and shared interests. Open the community area and read the description and rules before joining or posting.</p><h2>Before you join</h2><p>Check the community topic, who can take part and whether it has any extra rules. Some spaces may be official GidiReads communities, while others may be created by members.</p><h2>Post or reply</h2><p>Choose the relevant discussion area, write a clear message and check it before posting. Keep replies connected to the topic. Respect other members even when you disagree.</p><h2>Report a problem</h2><p>If a post or member makes the space unsafe or breaks the rules, use the report option when it is available. Include enough information for the moderation team to find the content.</p><h2>Access or posting is blocked</h2><p>A community may be private, paused or limited by its rules. Read the message shown on the page. Do not create another account to get around a restriction.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-public-profile-and-reading-lists',category:'Getting started',title:'Your public profile and reading lists',summary:'Choose what other members can see and keep the stories you save organised.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Your public profile helps other members recognise you. It may show your username, display name, biography, public badges, public reading lists and activity that you have chosen to share.</p><h2>Review your profile</h2><p>Open your profile and use Edit profile or Account settings to change the details the page lets you update. Check the public view after saving so you know what other people can see.</p><h2>Keep private details private</h2><p>Do not put your email password, sign-in codes, home address, payment details or other private information in your biography or public lists.</p><h2>Reading lists</h2><p>Use your Library and reading-list controls to organise stories. If a list has a public or private setting, check it before sharing. A private list should not be visible to other members.</p><h2>Badges</h2><p>If badge visibility settings are available, choose which earned badges you want to show. Hiding a badge does not remove the achievement from your account.</p>`},
{slug:'reader-report-content-or-a-profile',category:'Account and safety',title:'Report a story, comment or profile',summary:'How to report something that concerns you and what information to include.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Use the report option when a story, comment or profile appears to break GidiReads rules or creates a safety concern.</p><h2>Open the report option</h2><p>On the relevant story, comment or public profile, look for Report. Choose the reason that best matches the issue and follow the form shown on the page.</p><h2>Give useful details</h2><p>Explain what happened in a few clear sentences. Include the story title, username or comment location so the team can find it. Only share information needed to explain the report.</p><h2>What happens next?</h2><p>The platform team can review the report and take action under the platform rules. A report does not guarantee that content will be removed. The team may not be able to share private details about another member or the review.</p><h2>If you cannot use the report button</h2><p>Use the support or contact route shown on GidiReads. Do not post private evidence publicly or ask other members to target the account. If someone is in immediate danger, contact local emergency services.</p>`}
,{slug:'ae-editor-role-and-application',category:'Writing and publishing',title:'The Acquisitions & Developmental Editor role',summary:'What an AE does, how to apply and what happens after approval.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>An Acquisitions & Developmental Editor (AE) helps GidiReads find writers and supports authors who have been assigned to them. The role includes writer scouting, story feedback and promotion suggestions.</p><h2>How to apply</h2><p>Sign in and open Apply to be an AE in Authors Studio. Answer the short questions about your experience, editing approach, knowledge of GidiReads, sample feedback and weekly availability. Your application is sent to the admin team. You can only enter the AE Workspace after an admin approves your application.</p><h2>Application decisions</h2><p>The admin team can mark an application In Review, Approved or Rejected. Approval gives your account the AE role. A pending or rejected application does not give dashboard access. Applying does not guarantee a role.</p><h2>My Authors and Editorial Desk</h2><p>The admin team connects each AE to authors who have an active GidiReads contract. Your workspace only shows authors and contracted stories assigned to you. Open a story review to read its published chapters. The review page is read-only: it does not let you change or publish the author's work.</p><p>You can send feedback about story structure, a chapter or story continuity. Be clear and respectful. Explain what may confuse readers and suggest ways the author could improve it. The author can read your feedback in Authors Studio under Editorial Feedback and reply there. The author decides whether to use your suggestions.</p><h2>Author Scouting</h2><p>Add writers you find on other platforms. Record their pen name, where you found them, a public profile link, a suitable contact route and your notes. Update the lead as you make contact. Do not spam writers or promise a contract, income or a place on GidiReads.</p><h2>Promotions</h2><p>You can recommend an assigned story for the homepage, Explore, a collection, a campaign or an author spotlight. Explain why it may be a good fit. The admin team reviews every recommendation and makes the final decision. Sending a recommendation does not mean it has been approved.</p><h2>Performance</h2><p>Your workspace counts assigned authors, feedback sent, writers tracked and promotion decisions. These counts help you manage your work. They are not a measure of story quality and do not promise payment.</p><h2>What an AE cannot do</h2><p>An AE cannot approve or sign contracts, change an author's published manuscript, approve payments or earnings, or publish a major promotion without admin approval. Only the admin team can approve AE applications, assign authors and approve promotion requests.</p><h2>Respect and privacy</h2><p>Keep manuscripts, author details and application information private. Use only the information needed for your work. Never share private story content or contact details outside GidiReads. Authors must be free to accept or decline feedback.</p>`}

,{slug:'bulk-chapter-import',category:'Managing stories',title:'Import several chapters at once',summary:'How to prepare, review and save a batch of chapters as drafts.',updated:'2 November 2026',body:`<p>Bulk Chapter Import lets you bring several chapters into one Story Workspace. It is useful when you have written chapters in Word or another writing app.</p><h2>Prepare your files</h2><p>You can select text-based PDF, DOCX, TXT or Markdown files. Google Docs users can download their document as a Word file first. Older DOC files are not supported. PDF files must contain selectable text; scanned/image-only PDFs need OCR or conversion to DOCX/TXT before import. You can also paste a manuscript that contains several chapters.</p><h2>Split and check chapters</h2><p>The importer looks for clear chapter headings such as Chapter 1, Prologue or Epilogue. A line made of three or more dashes, stars or equals signs can also separate chapters. Check the preview because headings in your manuscript may not always be detected correctly.</p><h2>Review before saving</h2><p>Check every title and chapter preview. Change titles, move chapters into the right order, remove unwanted chapters or exclude any item you are not ready to import. The importer can flag exact duplicate text.</p><h2>What happens after import?</h2><p>Accepted chapters are saved as drafts. They are not published automatically, and the importer does not replace existing chapters. Read the import report and check your chapter list before leaving the page.</p><h2>File and batch limits</h2><p>Each file can be up to 10 MB, and one batch can contain up to 100 chapters. Word and PDF reading need an internet connection to load their document readers. TXT and Markdown files do not need those extra libraries.</p><h2>Keep a backup</h2><p>Keep your original manuscript in your writing app or device. Review the saved drafts in Story Workspace before you continue editing or publish.</p>`}


];


